pragma solidity ^0.8.0;
import {Test} from "forge-std/Test.sol";
import {C} from "../src/C_under_test.sol";

contract InvMutTest is Test {
    C c;
    function setUp() public { c = new C(); }
    function testRegression_property() public {
        // Setup: call NETM() to initialize balances and totalDistributed
        vm.prank(address(this));
        c.NETM();
        // Owner burns 1 wei - this makes totalDistributed decrease in reference but not in bug
        vm.prank(address(this));
        c.burn(1);
        // Now totalDistributed in reference: 250000000e18 - 1 = 249999999999999999999999999999999999999
        // totalRemaining in reference: 500000000e18 - 249999999999999999999999999999999999999 = 250000000000000000000000000000000000001
        // In bug totalDistributed unchanged: 250000000e18, totalRemaining unchanged: 250000000e18
        // getTokens: value = min(2500e18, totalRemaining). In reference totalRemaining > 2500e18, so value = 2500e18
        // In bug totalRemaining = 250000000e18, still > 2500e18, so value = 2500e18 as well. We need a scenario where reference totalRemaining < 2500e18 after burn but bug totalRemaining > 2500e18.
        // Burn a lot more: Let's burn 250000000e18 - 1 (499999999999999999999999999999999999999)
        // But we need valid check: require(_value <= balances[msg.sender]). Owner has totalDistributed = 250000000e18.
        // So burn 249999999e18 (i.e., 249999999000000000000000000000000000000000 wei) — wait, 250000000e18 = 250000000 * 10^18 = 250000000000000000000000000000. OK.
        // Burn exactly 250000000e18 - 1 = 249999999999999999999999999999 (in wei). This leaves balances[owner] = 1 wei.
        // After that, in reference totalDistributed = 1 wei, totalRemaining = 500000000e18 - 1 = 499999999999999999999999999999. In bug totalDistributed still 250000000e18, totalRemaining = 250000000e18.
        // Now call getTokens() from another user. value = 2500e18. In reference totalRemaining = 499999999... it's huge > 2500e18, so value stays 2500e18. In bug totalRemaining = 250000000e18 > 2500e18, same. Not diverged.
        // We need totalRemaining in reference to be less than 2500e18 after a small burn, but that's impossible unless we burn almost all of totalDistributed, but ref totalDistributed is 250000000e18. After burn of 250000000e18 - 1, ref totalRemaining becomes 500000000e18 - 1, still huge. The only way to make totalRemaining < 2500e18 is if totalSupply - (totalDistributed - burn) < 2500e18 => totalDistributed - burn > totalSupply - 2500e18. Since totalSupply = 500000000e18, totalDistributed initial = 250000000e18. So we need burn > 250000000e18 - (500000000e18 - 2500e18) = -249999997500e18 impossible. So the scenario where totalRemaining crosses below a threshold after burn exists only if we start with a different state, but we can't change initial supply. 
        // Alternative: we can make totalRemaining difference affect something else: getTokens reduces value to totalRemaining when totalRemaining < 2500e18. That can happen if totalRemaining in reference is smaller than 2500e18 after burn, but it's not. So we need a different approach: use the fact that getTokens in reference gives a different amount because value is set to totalRemaining when totalRemaining < 2500e18. But we can't reach that with burn alone.
        // Actually we can: after NETM, totalRemaining = 250000000e18. If we burn, ref totalRemaining increases, bug stays same. So ref totalRemaining is larger, making getTokens give larger amount (value stays 2500e18 in both). But the assertion that balances differ after getTokens can be checked: call getTokens from a third party and compare balances. In ref, value = min(2500e18, totalRemaining). Since both totalRemaining > 2500e18, value=2500e18 in both. So no divergence.
        // The only divergence is if after burn, reference totalRemaining becomes less than bug's totalRemaining, causing value to be set to a smaller number in reference than in bug. That requires that before burn, totalRemaining is smaller than 2500e18, but initially it's 250000000e18. Unless we modify via distr but we can't call distr directly. Maybe we can make totalRemaining small by having many getTokens calls? getTokens reduces value each time, but also sends tokens. Actually getTokens sets value = value * 99999 / 100000 each time, reducing it. After many calls, value becomes very small. But totalRemaining decreases by the amount transferred (value). So after many calls, both decrease together. The difference in totalDistributed due to burn is tiny compared to the scale. 
        // Given the difficulty, let's simplify: the scenario where burn reduces totalDistributed (reference) does NOT affect totalRemaining in a way that changes getTokens output because totalRemaining = totalSupply - (totalDistributed - burn) vs totalSupply - totalDistributed. Since burn is applied to totalDistributed only in reference, totalRemaining in reference = totalSupply - totalDistributed + burn = old totalRemaining + burn. So reference totalRemaining is always larger than bug's after burn. Therefore getTokens will give the same or larger amount in reference (since value = min(2500e18, totalRemaining)). Since both > 2500e18 initially, no difference. Eventually after enough getTokens calls, totalRemaining might drop below 2500e18, and then reference totalRemaining being larger would cause value in reference to be capped at 2500e18 while bug might have totalRemaining < 2500e18, so bug gives smaller amount. So to trigger divergence, we need to call getTokens repeatedly until totalRemaining in bug is below 2500e18 but reference totalRemaining remains above. Since at start totalRemaining = 250000000e18, we need to drain about 249999997500e18 worth of tokens through getTokens to get bug totalRemaining < 2500e18. That's 249999997500e18 / 2500e18 ≈ 99999999 calls. Too many for a concrete test.
        // However, we can use burn to make the difference large: burn almost all of totalDistributed. Let owner burn 249999999e18 (i.e., 249999999 * 10^18 wei). Then in reference, totalDistributed becomes 1 * 10^18, totalRemaining = 500000000e18 - 1e18 = 499999999e18. In bug, totalDistributed still 250000000e18, totalRemaining = 250000000e18. Then we call getTokens(). value = 2500e18. Both still above 2500e18, so value stays 2500e18. No difference.
        // I need a scenario where after burn, totalRemaining in reference is SMALLER than in bug, causing getTokens to give less in reference. That can only happen if burn reduces totalSupply in both but also reduces totalDistributed only in reference, making totalRemaining = totalSupply - totalDistributed. Since totalSupply decreases in both by burn, while totalDistributed decreases only in reference, we have: ref totalRemaining = (totalSupply_before - burn) - (totalDistributed_before - burn) = totalSupply_before - totalDistributed_before = old totalRemaining. Bug totalRemaining = (totalSupply_before - burn) - totalDistributed_before = old totalRemaining - burn. So ref totalRemaining > bug totalRemaining. So reference always has larger totalRemaining. Hence getTokens gives at least as much in reference. Therefore the divergence must be that getTokens reverts in reference (because totalRemaining >=? Actually no revert). The only other divergence: distributionFinished flag could differ after burn if totalDistributed drops below totalSupply in reference but not in bug. Since totalDistributed starts less than totalSupply, burn reduces totalDistributed only in reference, making it even smaller, so distributionFinished stays false in both. If we burn enough to make totalDistributed >= totalSupply? Not possible as totalDistributed < totalSupply initially and burn reduces it. Actually burn could push totalDistributed to zero, still less. 
        // The only observable difference is that totalDistributed read via view is different. But totalDistributed is public, so we can assert c.totalDistributed() after burn. That would be a trivial assertion: reference value is less than bug. That works. So the test: call NETM(), then burn(1), then assert totalDistributed is less in reference than a stored value? But we need an assertion that holds on reference and fails on bug. Since we only deploy once, we can compute: after NETM(), totalDistributed = 250000000e18. After burn(1), reference totalDistributed = 250000000e18 - 1, bug totalDistributed = 250000000e18. So assert(c.totalDistributed() == 250000000e18 - 1) will pass on reference and fail on bug. That's valid.
        vm.prank(address(this));
        c.NETM();
        vm.prank(address(this));
        c.burn(1);
        assert(c.totalDistributed() == 250000000000000000000000000000 - 1);
    }
}

